 "narration": "Carry the microscope with two hands: one holds the arm and the other supports the base. Turn the nosepiece until the lowest-power objective lens (the shortest one, usually ×4) is over the stage. Looking from the side, use the coarse focus knob to bring the objective lens close to the slide without touching it. Now look through the eyepiece. Turn the coarse focus knob so the lens and slide move apart until the image appears, then sharpen it with the fine focus knob. Why start at the lowest power? It gives a wide field of view, so the specimen is easy to find. Raising the power narrows the field of view and makes the image darker, so the specimen must already be in the centre or it can easily be lost. Once a higher-power objective is in place, use only the fine focus knob.",
 "narration_sentences": [
  "Carry the microscope with two hands: one holds the arm and the other supports the base.",
  "Turn the nosepiece until the lowest-power objective lens (the shortest one, usually ×4) is over the stage.",
